.TH TCPCAT 1 "November 2026" "tcpcat" "Network reconnaissance"
.SH NAME
tcpcat \- network scanner that shows what your servers expose
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tcpcat
.RI [ options ]
.I target
.RI [ target ... ]
.br
.B tcpcat ai
.RI { explain | policy | identify | model }
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tcpcat
scans your servers from the outside and tells what they expose, why it
matters and how to fix it. It is written in Go and intended for authorized
security assessments and network administration. It performs TCP and UDP
port scanning with host discovery, service and version detection, OS
fingerprinting and CVE correlation, explains the results with an offline
AI, and writes machine-readable reports.
.PP
The
.B tcpcat ai
subcommand runs the same analysis on a saved JSON report
.RB ( "tcpcat ai explain scan.json" ),
drafts an exposure policy
.RB ( "tcpcat ai policy" ),
names the service behind a banner
.RB ( "tcpcat ai identify" )
and shows what its model knows
.RB ( "tcpcat ai model" ).
.PP
Targets may be host names, IP addresses or CIDR ranges. Raw-socket scans
require root privileges or the
.B CAP_NET_RAW
capability.
.PP
On Linux, the optional
.B \-\-ebpf
engine attaches an XDP program at run time and redirects only the replies to
tcpcat's own probes into AF_XDP sockets; all other traffic on the interface
continues through the normal kernel network stack. When the driver offers no
zero-copy support (AF_XDP copy mode), tcpcat uses its raw-socket engine
instead, which is faster there, unless
.B \-\-ebpf\-copy
is given.
.SH OPTIONS
Only the most common options are listed here.
.B tcpcat \-\-help
prints a short help, and
.B tcpcat \-\-help\-all
the complete, authoritative list.
.SS Target and discovery
.TP
.BI \-i " interface"
Network interface to use, bypassing auto-detection.
.TP
.BI \-\-scope\-file " file"
Restrict scanning to the authorized CIDRs, IPs or domains listed in
.IR file .
.TP
.B \-Pn
Skip host discovery and treat every target as online.
.SS Port and scan type
.TP
.BI \-p " ports"
Ports to scan, for example
.B 80,443
or
.BR 1\-1024 ;
.B \-p\-
scans every port.
.TP
.BI \-\-top\-ports " n"
Scan the
.I n
most common ports.
.TP
.B \-sS
TCP SYN scan.
.TP
.B \-sT
TCP connect scan.
.TP
.B \-sU
UDP scan.
.TP
.B \-\-ebpf
Enable the AF_XDP/eBPF engine (Linux only). In copy mode the raw-socket
engine is used instead.
.TP
.B \-\-ebpf\-copy
Keep the AF_XDP engine even in copy mode.
.SS Detection
.TP
.B \-sV
Service and version detection.
.TP
.B \-O
Operating-system detection.
.SS AI analysis
.TP
.B \-\-ai
Let tcpcat's offline AI judge the results: what to fix, why, and how. Every
statement comes with its evidence.
.TP
.B \-\-ai\-deep
Thorough analysis. The AI verifies its hypotheses with read-only follow-up
probes and never sends a credential. It tells how the scan went and what
blocked it, works out what the machine is, and plans the fixes.
.TP
.BI \-\-ai\-lang " fr|en"
Language of the AI's answers (default: from
.BR $LANG ).
.TP
.BI \-\-ai\-from " internet|internal"
Where the scan runs from, for the AI (default: guessed).
.SS Timing and output
.TP
.BI \-T " 0\-5"
Timing template, from slowest and stealthiest to fastest.
.TP
.BI \-\-rate " pps"
Cap on packets per second (default: 1000).
.TP
.BI \-j " file"
Write results to
.I file
as JSON.
.TP
.BI \-\-sarif " file"
Write findings to
.I file
as SARIF 2.1.0.
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-log " file"
Append an audit record, one JSON object per line, to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-\-baseline " file"
Compare the current scan against a prior JSON report.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
See what a server exposes, verify it and plan the fixes:
.B tcpcat \-sT \-sV \-\-ai\-deep 203.0.113.10
.TP
Scan the 1000 most common ports across a subnet:
.B sudo tcpcat \-sS \-\-top\-ports 1000 192.168.1.0/24
.TP
Detect services and export JSON and SARIF for a single host:
.B sudo tcpcat \-sS \-sV \-O \-p 22,80,443 10.0.0.5 \-j report.json \-\-sarif report.sarif
.TP
Run a conservative, scope-limited assessment with an audit log:
.B sudo tcpcat \-\-profile safe\-production \-\-scope\-file scope.txt \-Pn \-sT \-sV \-p 443 \-\-audit\-log audit.jsonl 10.0.0.5
.SH SECURITY
tcpcat is intended solely for systems you own or are explicitly authorized to
test. Unauthorized scanning may be illegal. See
.B /usr/share/doc/tcpcat/SECURITY.md
for the vulnerability-disclosure process.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nmap (1),
.BR masscan (1)
.PP
Full documentation and source: https://tcpcat.io
